    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>An object that combines two iterators into a range suitable for use with C++11&apos;s range for. It is most conveniently used with the function range. For example:</para>
<para>std::vector&lt;int&gt; v; for (int x : range(v.begin(), v.end()) {}</para>
<para>std::vector already has begin and end members, so <ref refid="submodules_2mathicgb_2src_2mathicgb_2_range_8hpp_ac20b1ffc07c4258287b97217efa97a97_1ac20b1ffc07c4258287b97217efa97a97" kindref="member">range()</ref> is not necessary here. <ref refid="submodules_2mathicgb_2src_2mathicgb_2_range_8hpp_ac20b1ffc07c4258287b97217efa97a97_1ac20b1ffc07c4258287b97217efa97a97" kindref="member">range()</ref> is useful when a class does not have begin or end members, or if it offers several different ranges that can be iterated through - then the default range can only offer one of those ranges. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
